--TEST--
`query` takes its values beside the statement and a one-argument call is refused, so there is no spelling that leaves a placeholder unbound — `rule:core-classes/db-one-api` and `rule:core-classes/db-statement-members`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Db\Connection::query
// `rule:core-classes/db-one-api`: there is **no `prepare` step**. `Core\Db\Connection::query`
// takes the SQL and the parameters together, and the per-connection statement
// cache is what a separate `prepare` would have bought — so `PDO`'s two
// spellings of one operation are one member here, and the bound below is what
// a statement with nothing to bind passes.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");

// The last accepted call: an empty array is a statement with no placeholders,
// not a missing argument, and it is accepted.
var $none = $db->query("select 1", []);

// And the first refused one, which is `PDO::query($sql)` exactly. A member
// that defaulted the values would accept this and bind nothing, which is
// where every unbound placeholder in the PHP corpus came from.
var $bare = $db->query("select 1");
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0402]: expected 2 to 3 argument(s), found 1
   --> case.nvs:17:13
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
